  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Bullpen grind wins are measured in who closes the door, not in dramatics. Brady Basso lasted four innings, and then the game belonged to the arms. Blewett two scoreless, Medina and Harris one each, Alvarado shutting it down with the save. That is how 2\u20130 feels in September when the bats are quiet and five strikeouts pile up against four hits. Max Muncy went 2-for-2 and Henry Bolte's solo homer were enough because the pitching never fractured. The lead never moved after Toronto tied it in the second. This is not a formula. This is what survival looks like when your starter exits early and your offense doesn't have to do much.",
    "what_this_means": "The math here is narrow and repeatable only if the bullpen stays intact. They won by two runs on a day when eight runners crossed base via strikeout or empty at-bat. Muncy and Bolte carried the offensive load, but the real story is the absence of pressure. Nobody on the other side could break through after the early inning deadlock. They've now won two straight and cut the division gap by half a game, but wins built on pitching depth and minimal offensive demand expose a threshold: this works when the bullpen doesn't fatigue and the lineup doesn't have to carry the team. The moment one arm breaks, the margin disappears.",
    "what_to_watch": "George Kirby comes in with a 4.18 ERA, and Seattle's .229 batting average is inviting for Jeffrey Springs tonight.",
